  "id": "Compound::DB00835 - Gene::3269",
  "verbalization": "Brompheniramine, Captodiame, and Dexbrompheniramine share a common structural relationship, with all three being members of the histamine-1 receptor antagonist class. This class of compounds is known to interact with the histamine-1 receptor, a G-protein-coupled receptor involved in various physiological processes. The evidence suggests that these compounds, through their structural similarities, bind to Hrh1, a protein that likely plays a role in the signaling pathway of the histamine-1 receptor. The lowest common ancestors of these compounds—such as Histamine-1 Receptor Antagonist and Organic Cyclic Compound—highlight a shared pharmacological profile, reinforcing the idea that they are functionally related through their interaction with Hrh1. The structural similarities and the binding relationship between the compounds and Hrh1 suggest a mechanism of action that is consistent across these related molecules, positioning Hrh1 as a key target in the pharmacological action of brompheniramine and its derivatives.",
